---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';
import { QUIZ_DURATION } from '../../lib/quiz';

const guide = getGuide('scholarships-for-grade-12-students-alberta');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    Grade 12 scholarship season is not one deadline, it is a rolling series of them from September to June. Most students find out about the big fall awards in spring, after they have closed. This is the timeline that prevents that.
  </p>

  <h2>September and October: the big national awards open</h2>
  <p>
    The most valuable scholarships in Canada close earliest. The <a href="/scholarships/loran-scholarship/">Loran Award</a> (up to $100,000 over four years) typically closes in mid-October, and it needs a real application: essays, references, and activities going back years. If you are reading this in September of Grade 12, start Loran this week.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Make a list of every award you might apply to. <a href="/match/">The match quiz</a> takes {QUIZ_DURATION} and builds the list for you.</li>
    <li>Ask two teachers for reference letters now, before the November crush. Our <a href="/guides/reference-letters-for-scholarships/">reference letter guide</a> covers exactly how.</li>
    <li>Write one strong personal essay you can adapt. See <a href="/guides/how-to-write-a-scholarship-essay/">how to write a scholarship essay</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>November and December: community leadership awards</h2>
  <p>
    Awards like <a href="/scholarships/td-scholarships-for-community-leadership/">TD Scholarships for Community Leadership</a> close in this window. These reward sustained volunteering over one-off achievements. If your application is thin here, December is also when you can still start something real for the spring local awards.
  </p>

  <h2>January and February: institutional and nominated awards</h2>
  <p>
    University entrance scholarships mostly assess you automatically when you apply for admission, but the big named ones (like Schulich Leader, which requires a school nomination) have winter internal deadlines. Two things to do:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask your guidance counsellor which awards your school can nominate students for, and when the internal deadline is. Internal deadlines are always earlier than published ones.</li>
    <li>Apply for admission early. Some entrance awards only consider students admitted by a cutoff date.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>March to May: local awards, where the odds are best</h2>
  <p>
    Spring is when city bursaries, service clubs, community foundations, and employer awards all close. Individually they are smaller, often $500 to $1,000, but the applicant pools are tiny. A Medicine Hat award competing against a few dozen local students is a far better bet than a national award competing against thousands. Browse <a href="/scholarships/">the directory</a> filtered to your region, and read <a href="/guides/local-scholarships-better-odds/">why local awards are the best value</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>June and beyond: the Rutherford</h2>
  <p>
    After graduation, one more application is waiting: the <a href="/guides/alexander-rutherford-scholarship-guide/">Alexander Rutherford Scholarship</a>, worth up to $2,500 for your high school marks alone. You apply once you are enrolled in post-secondary. No essay, no competition. Do not forget it exists.
  </p>

  <h2>The one-line version</h2>
  <p>
    Big national awards in fall, nominations in winter, local awards in spring, Rutherford after graduation. Save the scholarships you plan to apply for and <a href="/saved/">turn on deadline reminders</a> so none of them pass silently.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
